t by improving water quality and filtration, removing carbon, varying the diet and reducing stress. When intestinal parasites are suspected, metronidazole is commonly used, following the product label or a vet’s directions."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Why is my flowerhorn floating upside down?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "That points to a swim bladder problem, often from overfeeding, constipation or poor water. Check the water, fast the fish for a few days, and see a vet if it doesn’t improve."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Why is my flowerhorn not eating?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Stress from a move, water problems or illness are the usual reasons. A newly arrived fish may refuse food for several days, but a settled fish that stops eating needs a water test and a close look for symptoms."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Can I use aquarium salt for a flowerhorn?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Aquarium salt is commonly used for ich and for mild stress or wounds, and flowerhorns generally tolerate it. Follow the label directions and remove it with water changes once treatment ends."}}]}]}</script>
