   <h2 id="s1">Why fish, and why water</h2>
   <p>Feng shui, the Chinese practice of arranging surroundings to encourage the good flow of energy, or <i>qi</i>, has long associated water with wealth. Moving, clean water is thought to gather and circulate good energy, which is why aquariums and fountains are fixtures in homes, shops and restaurants across Asia.</p>
+  <p class="tool-link">Working out where the tank goes? See <a href="/feng-shui/placement/">feng shui tank placement</a>, room by room.</p>
   <p>Fish carry their own meaning through language. In Chinese, the word for fish, <span lang="zh-Hant">魚</span> (<i>yú</i>), sounds the same as <span lang="zh-Hant">餘</span> (<i>yú</i>), meaning surplus or abundance. That is why &ldquo;may there be abundance every year&rdquo; is a New Year greeting and why fish appear on so much celebratory art. A healthy fish in clean water is abundance you can see.</p>
   <p>For decades the arowana, the &ldquo;dragon fish,&rdquo; was the ultimate lucky fish. The flowerhorn arrived in the 1990s as a new contender, and quickly became a rival.</p>
 </section>
