   <details><summary>What does &ldquo;flowerhorn&rdquo; mean?</summary><div><p>It is a loose translation of the Chinese name <span lang="zh-Hant">花羅漢</span> (huā luóhàn). &ldquo;Flower&rdquo; refers to the patterned markings on the body, and &ldquo;horn&rdquo; to the hump on the forehead, which keepers call the kok.</p></div></details>
   <details><summary>Why are flowerhorns considered lucky?</summary><div><p>In feng shui, fish and water are tied to abundance. The flowerhorn adds a head hump that resembles the domed forehead of Shou Xing, the God of Longevity, markings some owners read as characters or numbers, and red and gold colors associated with fortune. Our <a href="/feng-shui/">feng shui guide</a> covers the details.</p></div></details>
   <details><summary>Can a flowerhorn really recognize its owner?</summary><div><p>Keepers consistently report that flowerhorns treat their owner differently from strangers, greeting one at the glass and flaring at the other. Research has shown that at least some fish can tell human faces apart, and cichlids are among the most behaviorally complex fish in the hobby. See <a href="/pet-fish/">the fish that acts like a dog</a>.</p></div></details>
