tedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "There is no published data, but they are generally considered shorter-lived than standard flowerhorns, which commonly reach 10 to 12 years. Keepers often report health problems appearing in the 2 to 5 year range, though some fish live much longer."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What is the difference between short body and bonsai flowerhorns?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Both have a shortened body. Bonsai is usually used for the most compact, exaggerated fish, but sellers use the names inconsistently."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Can you make a flowerhorn short body by keeping it in a small tank?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "No. The short body shape is genetic and bred in. A small tank or poor diet only produces a stunted, unhealthy fish with normal proportions."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Why is my short body flowerhorn floating or sinking?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Buoyancy trouble is the most common short body problem, often linked to overfeeding, constipation or poor water quality on top of the crowded body shape. Test and change the water, fast the fish for a day or two, then feed small sinking meals, and see an aquatic vet if it doesn’t improve."}}]}]}</script>
