ilver-blue finish is a coat of pearling over a base color. As some silks mature, the coat thins in places and the red, orange or yellow base shows through, which is genetic and not usually a health issue."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Do Thai Silk flowerhorns get a big kok?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Most develop a smaller, firmer kok than Kamfa or Zhen Zhu fish. Silks with a square, Kamfa-type body can grow a larger head, but the strain is prized mainly for color."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Are Thai Silks harder to keep than other flowerhorns?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "They need the same setup as any flowerhorn, but many keepers find them less forgiving of cool or dirty water. Stable warm temperatures, frequent water changes and quarantine for new fish go a long way."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "How big do Thai Silk flowerhorns get?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Adults can reach about 12 inches (30 cm), and keepers often report slower growth than red strains. Plan on the same tank size as any adult flowerhorn."}}]}]}</script>
